<p>&#8220;Reading philosophy is valuable, but students can struggle to keep track of the reasoning. That’s why displaying the map of a philosophical argument can make the subject so much more accessible&#8212;especially to students new to the discipline.&#8221;<span id="more-58097"></span></p>
<p>Many philosophy instructors find argument-mapping an effective way of teaching reasoning.</p>
<p>If you&#8217;re interested in learning more about it, and trying out some software that you and students can use to map arguments, read the following guest post by <a href="https://sites.google.com/view/jonathanreidsurovell" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jonathan Surovell</a> (Texas State University) and <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/dona-warren/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dona Warren</a> (University of Wisconsin-Stevens Point).</p>
